      "text": "To find the area of \\( S \\), we need to understand the transformation and properties of the complex plane involved. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Define the Hexagon:**\n   The hexagon has a center at the origin and sides one unit apart. Since the sides are parallel to the imaginary axis and this hexagon is regular, each side length is \\(2\\) units (because the distance between opposite sides is \\(2\\) units). The vertices of the hexagon can be represented as the sixth roots of unity scaled by \\(2\\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\text{cis} \\left( \\frac{k\\pi}{3} \\right) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.\n   \\]\n   These vertices are:\n   \\[\n   2, 2\\text{cis}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = 2 + 2i, 2\\text{cis}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = 1 + i, 2\\text{cis}\\left(\\pi\\right) = -1 + i, 2\\text{cis}\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) = -1 - i, 2\\text{cis}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = 1 - i, 2\\text{cis}\\left(\\frac{6\\pi}{3}\\right) = 2 - 2i.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Transformation:**\n   The transformation \\( z \\mapsto \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the exterior of the hexagon \\( R \\) to the interior of a new region \\( S \\). This transformation maps points inside the unit disk to the exterior of the hexagon, and vice versa.\n\n3. **Area Calculation:**\n   The area of the original hexagon \\( R \\) can be calculated using the formula for the area of a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times 2^2 = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n   \\]\n   The area of the exterior region \\( S \\) is one-fourth of the area of the exterior of a unit disk because the transformation \\( z \\mapsto \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the unit circle to the exterior of the hexagon. The area of the exterior of a unit disk is \\(\\pi\\), so the area of \\( S \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } S = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\times 6\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{6\\sqrt{3}\\pi}{4} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}\\pi}{2}.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\( S \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}\\pi}{2}}\\).",
