    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We can represent the vertices of the hexagon as the complex numbers $z_k = \\text{cis} \\left(\\frac{k\\pi}{3}\\right),$ where $k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,$ and the center is at the origin. Due to symmetry, the vertices form a regular hexagon with side length 1. The distance from the center to any vertex is the radius of the circumscribed circle, which can be calculated using the geometry of the hexagon:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Radius} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{Period} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4\\pi}{3 - 1} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\pi = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the vertices of the hexagon can be represented as $z_k = e^{i \\frac{k\\pi}{3}}$.\n\nNext, we need to determine the region $R$ outside the hexagon. For a given complex number $z$, $\\frac{1}{z}$ will be inside the hexagon if $|z| \\geq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, and it will be outside the hexagon if $|z| < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Therefore, the region $S = \\{ \\frac{1}{z} \\ | \\ z \\in R \\}$ is the region on the inside of the hexagon, where $|z| < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nThe area of the hexagon is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the hexagon} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times 1^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe area of the region $S$ is the area of the full circle of radius $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ minus the area of the hexagon:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } S = \\pi \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\pi \\left(\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}\\right) - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{\\pi^3}{4} - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of $S$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi^3}{4} - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
