      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - We have a regular hexagon centered at the origin with opposite pairs of sides one unit apart.\n   - One pair of sides is parallel to the imaginary axis.\n   - We need to find the area of the region \\( S \\), which is the region outside the hexagon, and the image of this region when each complex number \\( z \\) in \\( R \\) is replaced by \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\).\n\n2. **Visualizing the Hexagon:**\n   - Consider a regular hexagon \\( H \\) with vertices at \\( 1, i, -1, -i, 1, -1 \\) in the complex plane.\n   - This hexagon has internal angles of \\( 120^\\circ \\).\n\n3. **Finding the Image of the Hexagon:**\n   - If we reflect the vertices of the hexagon across the real and imaginary axes, we can determine the transformation \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\).\n\n   - Reflecting \\( 1 \\to -1 \\), \\( i \\to -i \\), \\( -1 \\to 1 \\), \\( -i \\to i \\), \\( 1 \\to 1 \\), \\( -1 \\to -1 \\).\n   - The vertices of the image hexagon are \\( -1, -i, 1, i, -1, 1 \\).\n\n4. **Identifying the Region \\( S \\):**\n   - The region \\( S \\) is the entire complex plane that is not within the unit circle centered at the origin.\n   - This is the complement of the circle \\( |z| \\leq 1 \\).\n\n5. **Calculating the Area:**\n   - The area of the complex plane minus a disk of radius 1 is the area of the entire plane minus the area of the unit disk.\n   - The area of the unit disk is \\( \\pi \\times 1^2 = \\pi \\).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - Therefore, the area of \\( S \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\pi}\n   \\]",
