    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the area of region $S$, we first need to understand the geometry of the hexagon and its reflection.\n\nA regular hexagon inscribed in a circle of radius $r$ has side length $s$. For a hexagon with the center at the origin in the complex plane and one pair of sides parallel to the imaginary axis, the radius $r$ of the circumscribed circle is equal to the distance from the origin to the midpoint of one of the sides. In this case, the side length $s$ will be equal to the radius $r$.\n\nSince the distance from the origin to the midpoint of one side of the hexagon is $\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}$, and this distance is one unit, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}} = 1 \\implies s = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThe area of one hexagon with side length $s$ is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of hexagon} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} (\\sqrt{3})^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThe region $R$ is therefore the exterior of the hexagon. When we take the reflection of $R$ across the origin and outside of the hexagon, we get the region $S$. The area of the region $S$ is twice the area of the hexagon (since the reflection doubles the area of the exterior region):\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } S = 2 \\times \\text{Area of hexagon} = 2 \\times \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThus, the area of $S$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
